{"availability":"available","full":"tomerglick57/Jevstiller","name":"Jevstiller","description":"Distill a repeated Jev classification task into a local model, on the fly — same answers, your hardware.","category":"Productivity","language":"Python","stars":64,"forks":9,"created":"2026-09-21T11:33:55Z","updated":"2026-10-05T14:47:36Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://jevstiller.pages.dev","topics":["classification","distillation","jev","knowledge-distillation","llm","mlops","model-cascade","python","typesafe"],"demo":"https://jevstiller.pages.dev/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T14:47:08.020Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-07T14:47:08.020Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-07T14:47:08.020Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-07T14:47:08.020Z","stars":64,"forks":9}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Jev, distilled on the fly. Same call. Same answers. Your hardware.","Put Jevstiller in front of a repeated Jev classification call. At first every request still goes to Jev. From Jev's own answers — with their full probability distributions — it trains a small local model on your traffic, checks that the model agrees with Jev within a budget you set, and then answers most requests itself. Uncertain or novel input, and a permanent random audit slice, keep going to Jev.","You already call Jev. Run Jevstiller next to your service and point the SDK at it; nothing else changes:"],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/tomerglick57/Jevstiller/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"3bb0909c8d24c8f8d99e6adf94ee469f2daeb31923e59b0e9ab6a0ed10b2da82","extractedAt":"2026-10-07T14:47:08.283Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T14:47:08.283Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Jev, distilled on the fly. Same call. Same answers. Your hardware.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/tomerglick57/Jevstiller/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[{"kind":"api_key","status":"optional